module runtime.abi_shadow

/// Port of the `runtime/src/shadow.rs` thread registry + frame lists, via the
/// Win32 TLS builtins (`win_tls_alloc` / `win_tls_get` / `win_tls_set`) and the
/// process-heap builtins (`win_get_process_heap` / `win_heap_alloc` /
/// `win_heap_free`), the storage and allocator this language does not otherwise
/// have. Slice S3 of the live-GC qualification (native parities:
/// `register_thread`/`unregister_thread`/`insert_thread_locked`/
/// `ensure_registered_locked`, `push_frame`/`pop_frame` under the registry
/// lock, `pickle_gc_register_thread`/`pickle_gc_unregister_thread` ids from 1).
///
/// THE SHAPE. A `GcThread` node is a three-word raw-heap block `[frames, id,
/// next]` (native struct: `*mut ShadowFrame`, `u32`, registry link) linked into
/// the process registry list `shadowRegHead` (native `THREAD_HEAD`). The
/// thread's own handle lives in a Win32 TLS slot that holds the NODE POINTER
/// (native `CURRENT_THREAD`), not the frame head - `node[0]` is the frame
/// list head. `shadowNextId` is native `THREAD_COUNT` (ids from 1; a fresh
/// registration after an unregister gets the next value, monotonic forever).
/// Nodes are raw-heap blocks, so the Pickle collector can never reclaim them
/// - the exact position of native's `Box::into_raw` - and unregistering
/// (`pickle_gc_unregister_thread`) releases the block the way native's
/// `Box::from_raw` does.
///
/// THE LOCK. `shadowLock` serialises every mutation of and walk over
/// `shadowRegHead` and every thread's frame list, one owner word spin with
/// `win_sleep(0)` between retries (native holds `REGISTRY`, a real
/// `Mutex<()>`, for exactly the same scope: `push_frame`, `pop_frame`,
/// `register_thread`, `unregister_thread`, and the collector's whole shadow
/// walk). Two divergences are recorded:
///   - The spin is not a hardware atomic: in every qualified image this
///     runtime serves ONE OS thread (the nostd contract; `spawn` runs its body
///     inline), so two acquirers can never interleave and a plain
///     read/write on the owner word is observably identical to the mutex.
///     A future multithread image must harden this word first.
///   - The pop-time LIFO check cannot reach the panic channel from this leaf
///     (`writeStderr` sits above the collector), so a pop out of order is a
///     silent `win_exit(1)`, not native's panicked
///     "shadow stack pop out of order" message. The emitter always pops
///     exactly the frames it pushed, so the check is a debugger aid, not a
///     runtime contract.
///
/// THE UNREGISTER LAW. Native `pop_frame` also runs `ensure_registered`,
/// which has a same-thread consequence the plain ABI does not show: after
/// `pickle_gc_unregister_thread` the next push/pop on the SAME thread
/// creates a FRESH node (a new id - `shadowNextId` keeps advancing) with an
/// empty frame list. `win_heap_alloc` may hand back the exact block the
/// unregister freed, so a pointer-identity probe after such a sequence can
/// read the new node as if the old one survived; probes and suites must
/// discriminate through ids and registry walks, never through addresses.
/// And because each node carries the thread's LIVE frame list, unregistering
/// a thread whose frames are still in use tears the whole chain away - the
/// next pop trips the out-of-order check (native: panic; here: silent
/// `win_exit(1)`). The seam is therefore for END-OF-LIFE threads: either a
/// spawned worker finishing, or the process tail. No suite may unregister
/// the running harness thread.
///
/// WHEN THE CHAIN MUST BE READ. The collector walks EVERY registered thread's
/// frame list under the lock (native `gc.rs` trace holds `lock_registry()` and
/// walks `all_threads()`); the engine seeds its worklist per registry node
/// through `abiShadowRegistryHead()` between `shadowRegistryLockAcquire()`
/// and `shadowRegistryLockRelease()`. `abiShadowHead()` keeps the
/// per-thread head read (native `current_thread()`'s node frames) for the
/// callers that want only this thread's chain.
/// The lock-step build against `combined.lib` is NOT qualified: that library
/// still links the native collector, which walks the Rust `GcThread` registry
/// (`THREAD_HEAD` -> `GcThread.frames`) and cannot see a head that lives only
/// in Win32 TLS. A lock-step AOT program that allocates enough to trip the
/// adaptive GC threshold then takes a use-after-free on a live frame - observed
/// as 0xC0000005 on a depth-30 x 2000-iteration recursion with a live list. In
/// `abi_gc`-importing images the native collector never runs (the ported
/// `pickle_gc_collect` owns the cycle), so the split cannot surface there.
/// `collector.pkl` (the modelled collector) walks a `ShadowRegistry` model and
/// is the shape the ported collector follows.
///
/// WHY THE BODY STAYS ZERO-CELL. `x64.pkl::pushShadow` emits
/// `call pickle_shadow_push` only when `plan.ncells > 0` (line 864), and the
/// `popShadow()` call at the `Return` term is gated the same way (line 1590).
/// A body with no managed locals therefore cannot instrument itself. This
/// body's locals are all `int`, the touched globals are scalar `var`s, and the
/// TLS/heap/exit/sleep builtins deal in `Int` values only, so it has zero
/// managed cells. That is the property the 2026-10-03 attempt lacked - it
/// stored the chain in a managed list and re-entered itself until the stack
/// died. If this ever regresses, the failure mode is the same loud stack
/// overflow, not silent corruption.
///
/// THE LAYOUT IS THE REFERENCE'S. The emitter's frame pointer is the base of a
/// `ShadowFrame`: header is 16 bytes with `prev` at word 0 and `slot_count` at
/// word 2 (writer tracks them at `shadowBytes`, `shadowBytes - 8`), and slots
/// follow at `+16 + 8*i`. `frame[0]` is therefore exactly the reference's
/// `prev` field, and `frame[slot]` is never touched here - slot spilling is
/// `pickle_shadow_set` / `pickle_shadow_get`, which stay Rust and are exercised
/// only by a program whose safepoints spill managed locals.
///
/// TLS INDEX. Allocated lazily at the first `ensure-registered` and kept in a
/// scalar top-level `var`, which is image data valid from process entry - the
/// `abi_statics` `cellsBase`/`cellsCap` pattern, the only mutable module
/// storage a runtime body may reach. `-1` is spelled `TLS_OUT_OF_INDEXES`;
/// `TlsAlloc` never returns it, so it is a safe "not yet allocated" sentinel.
/// A freshly allocated index may legitimately be 0 and must not be mistaken
/// for unset.
///
/// THE JIT CAVEAT. Like `abi_exception`, this channel can split under
/// `pickle-cli run`: the JIT may execute the Pickle body while another side
/// still reads Rust state. It is safe for AOT builds and for the selflink.
/// The selflink image is the only one exercised end-to-end (no native
/// collector, so no GC can free a live frame); lock-step AOT covers the body's
/// mechanical correctness at small volumes but must not be pushed past the GC
/// threshold. Byte-parity probes therefore run against the selflink.

/// The Win32 TLS index holding this thread's `GcThread` node pointer, or
/// `-1` (`TLS_OUT_OF_INDEXES`) before the first push/registration.
var shadowTlsSlot: int = -1

/// Process registry head: the chain of `GcThread` nodes (native
/// `THREAD_HEAD`), each `[frames, id, next]` on the raw heap.
var shadowRegHead: int = 0

/// Id handed to the NEXT thread that registers. Native `THREAD_COUNT` starts
/// at 1 so 0 can mean "no thread"; `fetch_add` returns the old value, so ids
/// observed in the registry are exactly 1, 2, 3, ... in registration order.
var shadowNextId: int = 1

/// Registry owner word (native `REGISTRY` Mutex): 1 = an acquirer holds the
/// exclusive lock, 0 = unlocked.
var shadowLock: int = 0

/// Take the registry lock. Bounded spin between `win_sleep(0)` retries - the
/// Sleep(0) yields the time slice, so a future contended image cannot starve;
/// in the qualified single-thread contract the loop never runs twice.
fn shadowRegistryLockAcquire() {
    while (shadowLock == 1) {
        win_sleep(0)
    }
    shadowLock = 1
}

/// Release the registry lock.
fn shadowRegistryLockRelease() {
    shadowLock = 0
}

/// Ensure this thread is registered, assuming the lock is held (native
/// `ensure_registered_locked`): TLS -1 (unallocated) allocates the slot; TLS
/// slot 0 (no node) creates the node on the raw heap, links it, remembers the
/// TLS handle; an existing node is returned untouched. Every referenced node
/// word is written explicitly - `win_heap_alloc` does NOT zero. A failed node
/// allocation (null under commit pressure) releases the lock and exits, the
/// same divergence `abiShadowPop` takes for native panics - writes through
/// address 0 inside the lock would be the worst possible failure shape.
fn shadowEnsureRegisteredLocked() -> int {
    unsafe {
        if (shadowTlsSlot == -1) {
            shadowTlsSlot = win_tls_alloc()
        }
        var node = win_tls_get(shadowTlsSlot)
        if (node == 0) {
            var heap = win_get_process_heap()
            node = win_heap_alloc(heap, 0, 24)
            if (node == 0) {
                shadowRegistryLockRelease()
                win_exit(1)
            }
            unsafe {
                (node as *int)[0] = 0
                (node as *int)[1] = shadowNextId
                shadowNextId = shadowNextId + 1
                (node as *int)[2] = shadowRegHead
                shadowRegHead = node
                let _ = win_tls_set(shadowTlsSlot, node)
            }
        }
        return node
    }
}

/// ABI: push `frame` onto the current thread's shadow stack (native
/// `push_frame` under the registry lock, with auto-registration).
#[symbol("pickle_shadow_push")]
fn abiShadowPush(frame: *int) {
    unsafe {
        shadowRegistryLockAcquire()
        var node = shadowEnsureRegisteredLocked()
        frame[0] = (node as *int)[0]
        (node as *int)[0] = frame as int
        shadowRegistryLockRelease()
    }
}

/// ABI: pop `frame`, which the emitter guarantees is the current head of this
/// thread's stack (native `pop_frame`: registry lock, frames==frame assert,
/// then the head unsticks and the popped frame's `prev` clears). Out-of-order
/// pop takes the silent `win_exit(1)` divergence instead of native's panic
/// message (the leaf cannot reach `writeStderr`).
#[symbol("pickle_shadow_pop")]
fn abiShadowPop(frame: *int) {
    unsafe {
        if (shadowTlsSlot != -1) {
            shadowRegistryLockAcquire()
            var node = shadowEnsureRegisteredLocked()
            if ((node as *int)[0] != (frame as int)) {
                shadowRegistryLockRelease()
                win_exit(1)
            }
            (node as *int)[0] = frame[0]
            frame[0] = 0
            shadowRegistryLockRelease()
        }
    }
}

/// ABI-read: this thread's shadow-frame chain head (a `ShadowFrame` base), or
/// 0 when nothing has been pushed on this thread yet (native
/// `current_thread()` -> `GcThread.frames`).
fn abiShadowHead() -> int {
    unsafe {
        if (shadowTlsSlot == -1) {
            return 0
        }
        var node = win_tls_get(shadowTlsSlot)
        if (node == 0) {
            return 0
        }
        return (node as *int)[0]
    }
}

/// Lookup: the process registry head for a collector walk (native
/// `all_threads()`). Read and walk between the lock calls; the caller holds
/// the lock exactly as native's trace holds `lock_registry()`.
fn abiShadowRegistryHead() -> int {
    return shadowRegHead
}

/// Lookup: the number of registered thread nodes (counted by walking the
/// chain the way native's `all_threads()` walk would). Diagnostic/test seam.
fn abiShadowRegistryCount() -> int {
    var count = 0
    var node = shadowRegHead
    while (node != 0) {
        count = count + 1
        unsafe {
            node = (node as *int)[2]
        }
    }
    return count
}

/// ABI: register the calling OS thread with the collector and return its id
/// (native `pickle_gc_register_thread` -> `register_thread().id`, ids from 1).
/// Idempotent per thread like the reference's `ensure_registered`.
#[symbol("pickle_gc_register_thread")]
fn abiGcRegisterThread() -> int {
    unsafe {
        shadowRegistryLockAcquire()
        var node = shadowEnsureRegisteredLocked()
        shadowRegistryLockRelease()
        return (node as *int)[1]
    }
}

/// ABI: unregister the calling OS thread from the collector (native
/// `pickle_gc_unregister_thread`): unlink the node from the registry, detach
/// the TLS handle, then release the raw-heap block - native's
/// `unregister_thread` takes the registry lock for exactly this sequence and
/// drops the Box under it. A never-registered thread is a no-op like the
/// reference (node null check).
#[symbol("pickle_gc_unregister_thread")]
fn abiGcUnregisterThread() {
    unsafe {
        if (shadowTlsSlot == -1) {
            return
        }
        var node = win_tls_get(shadowTlsSlot)
        if (node == 0) {
            return
        }
        shadowRegistryLockAcquire()
        var cur = shadowRegHead
        var prev = 0
        while (cur != 0 && cur != node) {
            prev = cur
            cur = (cur as *int)[2]
        }
        if (cur == node) {
            if (prev == 0) {
                shadowRegHead = (node as *int)[2]
            } else {
                (prev as *int)[2] = (node as *int)[2]
            }
        }
        let _ = win_tls_set(shadowTlsSlot, 0)
        var heap = win_get_process_heap()
        let _ = win_heap_free(heap, 0, node)
        shadowRegistryLockRelease()
    }
}


